# ─── Line endings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# LF in the repo AND in every working tree, on every platform.
#
# Every target is Linux now, but the rule costs nothing and stops a clone made
# with core.autocrlf=true (any git configured on Windows) from checking out
# CRLF -- under bash a CRLF shebang line or a CRLF `fi` is a syntax error.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` then overrides autocrlf in
# both directions, so this holds without asking anyone to configure their git.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary, so no normalization is ever attempted on them.
*.ttf binary
*.png binary
*.zip binary

# claude/settings.json is written by Claude Code at runtime, through the symlink
# declared in deps.conf's [claude] section. model and modelSettings (/model and
# /effort) are per-machine and per-conversation preferences, so the clean filter
# in tools/clean-claude-settings.sh swaps them for claude/model-defaults.json on
# the way into git. Edit that file to change the committed defaults. Personal
# keys Claude Code writes back (auto mode, the skip* prompt records, theme) are
# deleted on the way in; the list is in the filter.
#
# The filter itself is registered by claude/deps.sh: filter.* config lives in
# .git/config and cannot be committed, so this line alone does nothing until
# `make install claude` has run. That is the safe direction -- an unregistered
# filter is silently skipped rather than erroring.
claude/settings.json filter=claude-settings
